WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:08.320
While it might seem a little extreme to
put a whole datacenter at the bottom of ocean,

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.400
datacenters strike at the core of what this company does, at what Microsoft does.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.080
It is a kind of a far-out idea,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.920
but highly relevant to the future of Microsoft.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.200
And as we look at the world today, we see many more

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.500
interactive applications, things like AR and VR.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.660
In order to really address that and provide this more responsive cloud, one of the things we look at

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.960
is bringing the cloud closer to our customers.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.640
Project Natick, not only is it exercising green tech,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:47.220
but it's also an exercise in whether we can get to a mass production concept.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.620
The leadership in offshore renewable energy is really in Europe.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.060
Scotland is also home to the European Marine Energy Centre,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.980
which is the largest test site in the world for offshore renewable energies like tide and wave energy.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.240
Putting something the ocean is something that we don't really have a whole lot of experience with.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.180
For this deployment, we're working with a company called Naval Group of France.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.020
Getting that group of engineers in the same room with our group of engineers,

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.980
you know, there were ideas that came up and that spur an idea in someone else,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.440
it really led us to get to the design that we right now.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.260
The vessel was built as two pieces.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.280
There's an external shell which is the pressure

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.000
vessel itself and then an internal payload.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.320
So this datacenter runs entirely off renewable energy.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.020
Our vessel is powered by a combination of solar power,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.940
wind power, and offshore tidal and wave energy.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.960
This is a true lights out datacenter.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.580
People have been wanting to design such
a datacenter for the last decades that

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.160
I've been involved in the business,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.380
and this is the very first one

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.080
that I've actually seen that we're going to do it.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.780
And we'll see what happens.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.680
So we had around 35 people on the sea, five boats.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.860
And on a different crew, we had the team checking

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.340
all the electricity to make sure that the
connections were just perfect.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:41.600
(off-camera dialogue among crew)

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:51.420
(off-camera dialogue among crew)

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:59.000
The Natick datacenter by no means replaces those big datacenters we have come to know.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.680
Just being able to order precisely the
amount of datacenter capacity you need

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:12.760
and have it delivered to you, that is the product concept that we dream about with Project Natick.

